Spawn, mission 4, objective 131 [performance]: an owner's gated push that changes the UI's dependencies is judged on them. Before the gate, the integrator installs the candidate's own dependency set into the judging's tree, from the host's pnpm store and offline. A set the store lacks is a no-verdict that says "dependencies not in the store", and the gate never starts.

Why. K2 (@hugeicons 4.4.0 and its lockfile) was refused twice as no-verdict (requests 148307 and 148311). integrate/owner.rs `controller_modules` gives an owner judging's tree the installed controller's web/node_modules only when that set's .pnpm/lock.yaml equals the candidate's web/pnpm-lock.yaml. So any lockfile change leaves the tree without node_modules. check-web.sh declares `needs: controller:web/node_modules/`, the gate's preflight cannot judge it (exit 6), and 119.sh cannot build the UI either. Every owner push that changes a dependency gets no verdict, again and again. A stopgap of renaming a staged set into the tree, polled at 0.1 s, lost the race: the gate starts about 2 s after the checkout.

Read: crates/spawn-cli/src/integrate/owner.rs (`controller_modules`, `judge_owner_candidate`, `no_verdict_kept` and its P27 rule), deploy/controller.sh `deps` (the same install, online, for a deps tree), deploy/on-landing.sh (main's web/ install after a landing), rsi/scenarios/check-web.sh, and integrate/database_tests.rs `an_owner_judgings_tree_gets_the_controllers_web_node_modules`.

Required behaviour.
- When the controller has a web/node_modules set and the candidate's web/pnpm-lock.yaml equals its .pnpm/lock.yaml: copied, as today.
- When the two differ: the candidate's own set is installed into the tree's web/, before the room check and the gate.
  - Command: `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts --offline --ignore-pnpmfile --config.modules-dir=node_modules --config.virtual-store-dir=node_modules/.pnpm --config.manage-package-manager-versions=false --config.package-import-method=clone-or-copy --reporter=append-only`, run in the tree's web/, with stdin closed:
    - no network, and no package's lifecycle script;
    - no pnpmfile: a candidate's .pnpmfile.cjs, or a `pnpmfile:` in its pnpm-workspace.yaml, would run in pnpm even with --ignore-scripts (rev-g, F1 on R1);
    - pnpm's directories pinned inside web/ over anything a configuration says, so a modulesDir or virtualStoreDir cannot write outside the tree or into the controller's set;
    - no switching to another pnpm version;
    - no file of the tree sharing an inode with the store, so a candidate's scenario cannot write into the host's store.
  - pnpm runs with a cleared environment: PATH (the gate environment's, where the integrator's unit puts ~/.local/bin), HOME (the store's place) and LC_ALL=C, nothing else. Never the integrator's database URL or tokens. No new crate, no npm dependency.
- Before pnpm starts, and before the copy, the candidate's tree is read. Anything outside the reviewed shape is a no-verdict that begins "the candidate's web/ holds pnpm configuration an owner judging does not install with", then says what was found:
  - web/ that is a symbolic link (git stores links; the copy or the install would go where it points);
  - an .npmrc, .pnpmfile.cjs, .pnpmfile.mjs or pnpmfile.cjs in web/, or an .npmrc, pnpmfile or pnpm-workspace.yaml at the tree's root;
  - web/package.json, pnpm-lock.yaml or pnpm-workspace.yaml that is not a regular file;
  - in web/pnpm-workspace.yaml, a top-level key other than minimumReleaseAgeExclude (main's file holds that one alone), a top-level line that is no plain key, a first setting that is indented, or a tab indent. This refuses pnpmfile, modulesDir, virtualStoreDir, storeDir, configDependencies, hooks and every key pnpm adds later;
  - in web/package.json, a field outside name, version, private, type, description, license, scripts, dependencies, devDependencies, optionalDependencies and peerDependencies (so no pnpm, packageManager, devEngines or engines);
  - in web/package.json's dependencies, devDependencies, optionalDependencies or peerDependencies, a field that is not an object, or a dependency that is not the registry's: a range, version or dist-tag alone, so any ":" or "/" is refused (file:, link:, workspace:, portal:, git, github:, http(s):, an npm: alias, a path). rev-g, F2 on R2: `"x": "file:<a host directory>"` with the lockfile pnpm makes for it installed offline with the lock equal, and copied that directory (~/.config, say) into web/node_modules, where the candidate's own scenarios read it in the gate; `link:` made a symbolic link to it;
  - in web/pnpm-lock.yaml (the same rule, since a lockfile can say `version: link:<dir>` under a registry specifier): a `resolution` that is not `resolution: {integrity: …}` alone (so no tarball:, directory: or git), however the key is spelled; a value that starts with file:, link:, portal:, workspace:, git:, git+, github:, gitlab:, bitbucket:, http:, https: or npm:; such a scheme after a package key's "@"; a complex key (?), merge key, anchor, alias or tag; or a backslash or double quote anywhere (rev-g, F3 on R3: a YAML double-quoted scalar spells any key or value with escapes, `version: "\x66ile:…"` or `"r\x65solution": {directory: …}`, which pnpm decodes and no text rule reads; pnpm writes neither, and main's, K2's and Supply's lockfiles hold none). The rule reads text and parses no YAML. The settings line `excludeLinksFromLockfile: false` and names such as get-caller-file or terminal-link pass. main c62e620f and K2 3e08fb12 pass every rule (checked with web_config_refusal on their trees).
  A key or field is named only when it is a plain word. Widening a list is the owner's change.
  The rule has a second copy: scripts/web-install.sh --strict=web (objective 134), which the owner's own installs run (deploy/on-landing.sh for main's web/). A change to one is a change to both; 134's test runs the helper against this scenario's bad trees.
  - Afterwards, the set's node_modules/.pnpm/lock.yaml must equal the candidate's web/pnpm-lock.yaml, byte for byte (the comparison that gives the controller's set). Otherwise there is no verdict.
  - The result is a real directory, made before the gate starts, so the gate binds it at its setup as it binds a copy.
- When the install fails, the judging has no verdict through `no_verdict_kept`: recorded as source.push_no_verdict, with pnpm's output kept as the judging's log. So a second no-verdict for the same reason refuses the request (P27), never retried without end. The gate does not start.
  - A package the store lacks: pnpm 12 prints a wrapper code first (ERR_PNPM_META_FETCH_FAIL) and the miss as its cause (ERR_PNPM_NO_OFFLINE_META or ERR_PNPM_NO_OFFLINE_TARBALL). Any ERR_PNPM_NO_OFFLINE code anywhere in the output gives a reason that begins "dependencies not in the store". It is the same words every time, and it says the owner installs the set with network and requests again.
  - Another pnpm failure: "the candidate's web dependencies could not be installed: pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --offline failed (<pnpm's first ERR_PNPM code, or exit N>)".
  - pnpm that does not start: "… pnpm did not start (<error kind>)".
  - The reason holds no path that changes between judgings (P27 compares reasons).
- The controller's own web/node_modules is never written.
- A fleet task's gate is not changed, and the gap there takes another shape:
  - its `$controller` is the installed controller, whose web/node_modules is main's set, installed by deploy/on-landing.sh after each landing;
  - check-web.sh refuses a fleet task's dependency change unless main's web-dependencies-reviewed names that exact set;
  - even a reviewed set would be built against main's packages, so a new package fails the build: a rejection, not a no-verdict.
  Dependency changes come through owner pushes, which this objective covers. After K2 lands, on-landing.sh installs main's new set and fleet gates use it.

Files (expected; no other open objective touches them): crates/spawn-cli/src/integrate/owner.rs and crates/spawn-cli/src/integrate/database_tests.rs. One crate. The throwaway is +821 −19:
