Unit tests:
- integrate/owner.rs, `a_workspace_file_holds_mains_one_key_alone`, `a_package_json_holds_plain_fields_alone` (registry ranges, dist-tags and scoped names pass; file:, link:, github:, npm:, workspace:, a path, a number and a non-object field are refused) and `a_lockfile_resolves_from_the_registry_alone` (a lockfile with the settings line and file/link-named packages passes; each lockfile shape above is refused, the escaped value, package key and resolution key, a plain double quote and a bare backslash included): main's files pass, and each refused shape above is refused.
- integrate/database_tests.rs (on the test database), with a stub pnpm first on the gate environment's PATH:
  - `an_owner_judgings_tree_gets_the_controllers_web_node_modules`, which keeps its name (no test is retired):
    - the controller's set when the lockfiles match: copied, and pnpm is not run;
    - other dependencies: pnpm runs once with exactly `INSTALL_ARGS` and the environment names HOME, LC_ALL and PATH alone; the gate finds the installed set; the push lands; the controller's set is untouched;
    - a set the stub fails as pnpm 12 does (META_FETCH_FAIL, then NO_OFFLINE_META): no gate, and the no-verdict is `NOT_IN_STORE`;
  - `an_owner_judging_refuses_web_config_that_could_run_code`: a web/.pnpmfile.cjs, a pnpm-workspace.yaml with modulesDir, and a package.json with a `file:` dependency, each get the `WEB_CONFIG` no-verdict naming it; pnpm never starts and no gate runs.

No polled read changes: the install runs once per owner judging, before its gate, only when the lockfiles differ (about 1.6 s offline for today's web/ set, 145 MB).

Acceptance: rsi/scenarios/131.sh. Black box, on its own database, with a recording gate (SPAWN_GATE). pnpm's store is the run's scratch HOME's, never the host's (checked). The packages are made up, served by a registry of the run's own on 127.0.0.1 (a python -I script in the run's scratch) that fills the store and makes the lockfiles, then stops before anything is judged, so every judging installs offline and the lockfiles resolve by integrity alone, as a real registry's do.
(1) A gated owner push whose lockfile adds made-up-b: one `spawn integrate --once`; the gate runs once; at its start the tree's web/node_modules is a real directory holding made-up-a and made-up-b, whose .pnpm/lock.yaml is the candidate's lockfile, with no file sharing an inode with the store; the push lands.
(2) The controller's web/node_modules is untouched.
(3) A gated owner push whose lockfile names made-up-absent@1.0.0 from the registry: one pass; the gate does not run; the request's source.push_no_verdict begins "dependencies not in the store"; main does not move.
(4) A second pass: the request is refused with verdict no_verdict; still no gate.
(5)-(8) Each pushed gated and given two passes: (5) a web/.pnpmfile.cjs; (6) `pnpmfile: hook.cjs` in web/pnpm-workspace.yaml, with the hook; (7) `modulesDir: ../../outside-modules`; (8) web/ committed as a symbolic link to a directory outside the tree. The hooks would write a marker with the environment's names. For each:
- the marker is never written;
- nothing named outside-modules exists under the run, and the link's target gets no node_modules;
- no gate runs;
- the no-verdict begins "the candidate's web/ holds pnpm configuration" and names what it found;
- the second pass refuses the request, and main does not move.
(9)-(10) A dependency on a directory of the host's (a stand-in for ~/.config holding a marker), each pushed gated and given two passes: (9) web/package.json's `"secretpkg": "file:<that directory>"` with the lockfile pnpm makes for it; (10) package.json of the registry's made-up-a and made-up-b, with the lockfile's made-up-b `version: link:<that directory>`. For each: the same no-verdict, naming the dependency or the lockfile's value; no gate runs; the second pass refuses the request; the marker is copied nowhere, and nothing links to the directory. (11) As (10), with the lockfile's value spelled through a YAML escape, `version: "\x6cink:<that directory>"`: the same, naming "a backslash or a double quote".
When an earlier case has landed and left main's web/ no directory (a base without the refusal lands (8)), (9)-(11) are a failed check, not a harness error.
The scenario exits 6 (cannot judge here) when no pnpm is on PATH or beside node. It never declares `needs: tool:pnpm`: a missing pnpm would then end every gate.
On main 60cf3d59, 131.sh exits 1 with 20 checks failed, the first "(1) at the gate's start the tree's web/node_modules was [none], not [dir a b lock own] …", then (3), (4), every case of (5) to (8), and "(9)-(11) not run: main's web/ is no directory (an earlier case landed)" (the base lands (8)'s symbolic link).

